[s0l0!-header][h1]The Return Type[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]return [/b]keyword can be used in methods to return a value.
For example, we could define a method named [b]sum [/b]that returns the sum of its two parameters.[code format="java" ]static [b]int [/b]sum(int val1, int val2) {
   [b]return [/b]val1 + val2;
}[/code]
Notice that in the method definition, we defined the[b] return type[/b] before we defined the method name. For our sum method, it is [b]int[/b], as it takes two parameters of the type [b]int [/b]and returns their sum, which is also an [b]int[/b].
[note]The [b]static [/b]keyword will be discussed in a future lesson.[/note]
Now, we can use the method in our main.[code format="java" codeId="755"]class MyClass {

static int sum(int val1, int val2) {
  return val1 + val2;
}

  public static void main(String[ ] args) {
    int x = [b]sum[/b](2, 5);
    System.out.println(x);
  }
}
[/code]
As the method returns a value, we can assign it to a variable.
[note]When you do not need to return any value from your method, use the keyword [b]void[/b].
Notice the [b]void [/b]keyword in the definition of the main method - this means that main does not return anything.[/note]

